 or bi-weekly for the  first 12 months because  remember that the incidence of PT LD is  greatest during that first year post  transplantation and a d+ r- would  represent a higher risk group but in  low-risk individuals such as when the  serial status is determined be d- r-  patients can be tested monthly for the  first year as we discussed on the  previous slide and elevated EBV viral  load doesn't necessarily mean that a  patient has or will develop PT L D so in  order to confirm the diagnosis you need  other studies including imaging so MRI  PET scans but ultimately you need a  tissue biopsy with histopathology and  specific staining for EBV so let's  return to our second case and see how  things turned out  so given the patient's elevated EBV  viral load and concerning findings on  the MRI and PET scans the transplant  team reduced the levels of  immunosuppression and also initiated  weekly rituximab therapy and you can see  in the table that the EBV viral loads  were followed weekly and showed a  significant decrease over the following  month and the virus actually became  undetectable by week five so a very  substantial response to initiation of  therapy in this patient to summarize a  few diagnostic pearls from case number  two quantitative viral load testing is  important a laboratory tool on cases of  EBV associated PT LD but it's important  to remember that high viral loads aren't  diagnostic for this door this disorder  in addition although the majority of  cases of PT LD are associated with EBV  infection there are some cases that have  very low levels or even undetectable EBV  and therefore those cases may be caused  by some other etiology  our third and final case is that of a 33  year old who underwent a kidney  transplant due to uncontrolled diabetes  and the patient does well during their  first 12 months post transplant and then  is seen for a routine follow-up  appointment where the patient has an  elevated serum creatinine level and then  a urinalysis  reveals pyuria and he materia so a  number of microbiology studies are  performed and notably the patient's BK  viral load on a urine sample is reported  as positive at 22,500 copies per ml a  urine cytology is performed at that time  and shows decoy cells similar to those  that you can see in the image in the top  right hand corner of your slide in  addition a kidney biopsy is stained  using an simian virus forty probe or  sv40 and that shows positive staining  that i've circled and read in multiple  fields so this is BK virus which is a  polyomavirus and it's related to simian  virus 40 or sv40 and that's why the SP  40 probe led up on the kidney biopsy BK  virus was named after a patient from  whom the virus was first isolated in the  early 1970s it's an on envelope virus  it's got a double-stranded DNA genome  and primary infection is believed to  occur during childhood through contact  with oral or respiratory secretions now  the majority of individuals become  exposed to this virus and so serial  prevalence studies suggest that between  60 and 80% of adults have been infected  with BK virus  following acute infection which in many  cases is believed to be asymptomatic the  virus then preferentially persists in  the renal epithelium BK virus can be  associated with significant more about  morbidity and mortality and the  transplant population among kidney  transplant recipients the virus can  cause tubular nephritis or urethral  stenosis and in the stem cell transplant  population BK virus is associated with  hemorrhagic or non hemorrhagic cystitis  less commonly it can cause other  illnesses such as meningoencephalitis  retinitis pneumonitis or hepatitis now  unlike testing for CMV and EBV serologic  screening for BK is not routinely  performed prior to transplantation and  also it's important to point out that  viral culture for BK is not commonly  done instead the diagnosis and  management of BK virus and the  transplant population relies on a  combination of histopathology and  quantitative viral load testing so in  regards to viral load testing urine and  plasma are the most common sample types  that are tested with detection of BK  virus and urine being associated with  high sensitivity and a high negative  predictive value so if you get a  negative result from a urine sample that  has a strong correlation that the  patient does not have BK a related  disease unfortunately though detection  of BK in the urine has low clinical  specificity so many transplant patients  will have detectable BK virus in their  urine but not have disease caused by  that virus in contrast to urine  detection of the  and plasma is felt to have much higher  clinical specificity and plasma is  typically used for monitoring patients  after a urine sample is initially  determined to be positive for BK virus  among the kidney transplant population  the current guidelines recommend  screening patients with a quantitative  molecular assay each month for the first  nine months post transplant again urine  can be used to screen patients but if  urine is positive for BK virus then  testing by plasma is recommended for  monitoring over time if a patient  remains negative by the viral load assay  for the first nine months screening can  then be performed every three months  until the patient's two years out from  their transplantation consistently  elevated or rising BK viral load should  be followed up by a kidney biopsy with  specific insight to hybridization  studies although there are no clear  universally applied thresholds to define  BK v disease it has been suggested that  viral loads of at least a thousand  copies and plasma over a time of three  weeks or a single value that's greater  than 10,000 copies should prompt the  team to consider a reduction in immune  suppression but those values really need  to be validated at each Center in the  stem cell transplant population a  diagnosis of BK associated hemorrhagic  cystitis requires a 